    "rerun_verdict": "Scope-limited (a) on both bases. BASELINE (lambda_A, reproduced): S_emp sign-flips across the series (sign_flip=True) — negative at p/n, large positive at A>=2 — so no uniform-coefficient substrate form exists; the PR #1876 negative reproduces. PER-KNOT (tr_M(K), canonical): the sign-flip DISAPPEARS (sign_flip=False; S_emp > 0 for all six atoms) and the best normalisation column S/(A*m_e) is uniform to a factor 1.33 (vs sign-flip = unbounded on the lambda_A base) — the p!=n-distinct canonical base is strictly MORE regular. HOWEVER the additive term is no longer sub-leading: max |S/m(K)| = 99.8% (the per-knot leading term is far below the mass at A>=2), so 'S(K) as a small closed-form correction' fails on the canonical base too — S absorbs most of the mass. VERDICT: the original hard shape of the refutation (sign-flipping, wildly non-uniform S) is PARTIALLY AN ARTIFACT of the lambda_A base (the canonical base removes the sign flip and compresses the spread to ~x1.3), but the closure itself remains open: no uniform-coefficient substrate form matches all six atoms on either base. Scope-limited negative (a) — constrains the additive single-term closure on these two bases only; not a structural exclusion of tr_M(K) or of richer multi-term closures."
